# Sunscreen Use

## Row {heigh="60%"}

### Regular Sunscreen Use by Gender

```{r}
#| echo: false
#| fig-height: 3.5
#| fig-width: 5.5

sunscreen_gender <- dashboard_data %>%
  filter(
    !is.na(gender),
    !is.na(regular_sunscreen)
  ) %>%
  count(gender, regular_sunscreen) %>%
  group_by(gender) %>%
  mutate(percent = n / sum(n) * 100) %>%
  ungroup()

ggplot(
  sunscreen_gender,
  aes(
    x = gender,
    y = percent,
    fill = regular_sunscreen
  )
) +
  geom_col(position = "dodge") +
  labs(
    title = "Regular Sunscreen Use by Gender",
    x = NULL,
    y = "Percent",
    fill = "Sunscreen Use"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    plot.title = element_text(size = 13),
    axis.text = element_text(size = 9),
    axis.title = element_text(size = 10),
    legend.title = element_text(size = 9),
    legend.text = element_text(size = 9)
  )
```

### Sunscreen Use Summary

```{r}
#| echo: false

sunscreen_gender %>%
  filter(regular_sunscreen == "Regular") %>%
  select(gender, percent) %>%
  mutate(
    percent = paste0(round(percent, 1), "%")
  ) %>%
  rename(
    Gender = gender,
    `Regular Sunscreen Use` = percent
  ) %>%
  kable()
```

# Predicting

## Row {height="45%"}

### Model Performance

```{r}
#| echo: false

model_performance <- tibble(
  Model = c("Big Model", "Small Model"),
  RMSPE = c(157.152, 165.532),
  MAE = c(100.461, 104.731),
  `Validated R²` = c(0.138, 0.046)
)

model_performance %>%
  knitr::kable(
    digits = 2,
    caption = "Test Data Performance"
  )
```

### Key Finding

The Big Model had lower prediction error and a higher $R^2$ than the Smaller Model. However, both models had significant prediction errors, which suggests that education and the demographic and sunscreen variables explain only a limited amount of variation in outdoor time.

# About the Data

## Row {height="50%"}

### Data

This dashboard uses data from the **NHANES 2017–March 2020 Pre-Pandemic cycle**. The analysis focuses on adults ages 20–59 who completed the examination.

The dashboard examines outdoor time on work or school days, education level, gender, and regular sunscreen use.

### Variables

**Outdoor Time:** Minutes spent outdoors on a typical work or school day.

**Education:** Less than high school, High school/GED, Some college/AA, or College graduate or above.

**Gender:** Male or Female.

**Regular Sunscreen Use:** Participants who reported using sunscreen always or most of the time were classified as regular sunscreen users.

